WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.583
(jaunty music)

00:00:03.713 --> 00:00:05.117
- Where's she?

00:00:05.117 --> 00:00:09.349
- Oh, you see Daniel Quan?

00:00:09.349 --> 00:00:10.834
You see Janet right there?

00:00:10.834 --> 00:00:12.143
She right there. She right there.

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:15.320
Hey, what's going on everybody?

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:19.083
We're gonna try to follow up Angela Patey.

00:00:22.874 --> 00:00:27.390
And forgive us my counterpart
to great Chloe Zhao.

00:00:27.390 --> 00:00:28.230
She's a little under the weather.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:29.373
She's not contagious.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:33.153
But I'm gonna do the lion's
share of the talking,

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:35.100
if that's okay.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:36.273
So, and bear with me,

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:38.730
we gotta talk about somebody who we love,

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:42.390
a great deal and we gotta
talk through a little bit

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:44.463
of a broken heart.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:50.663
So I ask for y'all, grace a little bit.

00:00:52.508 --> 00:00:55.258
(crowd applause)

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:05.130
So I first met Michelle
Satter over the phone,

00:01:05.130 --> 00:01:07.740
November 17th, 2001.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:09.780
It was a conference call.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:11.160
And I had learned that I was a finalist

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.050
for the Sundance Screenwriters Lab

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:15.630
that was taking place a few weeks later

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:19.710
on that next January before
the Film Festival in 2012.

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:21.690
And I was incredibly nervous.

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:23.280
I had heard about this program.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:24.960
All of the filmmakers I admired had been

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:25.793
through this program.

00:01:25.793 --> 00:01:27.000
They had been nurtured there.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:27.930
I wanted to be a part of it.

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:29.310
I wanted to be accepted.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:30.741
I was working on my film "Fruitvale"

00:01:30.741 --> 00:01:35.163
and I was feeling really
like it was a big moment.

00:01:36.374 --> 00:01:38.518
Chloe, you know this feeling?
- Yeah.

00:01:38.518 --> 00:01:42.150
- And I got on the call
and folks were talking,

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:44.100
and all of a sudden I heard this voice.

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:47.790
It was like a magical voice

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:50.340
because immediately soon as
this person started talking,

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:51.963
all my anxiety faded away.

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:54.510
It was incredible.

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:57.030
And I was able to speak
clearly and think clearly.

00:01:57.030 --> 00:01:58.440
And then as soon as we got off the call,

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.120
all the anxiety returned.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:05.120
And I felt like I blew the
call and I blew my moment.

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:08.010
And I was amazed to hear

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:11.022
that I had been accepted a few days later.

00:02:11.022 --> 00:02:14.880
And I was off to the right to this Lab.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:18.651
I arrived at the Lab
and I think I was late.

00:02:18.651 --> 00:02:22.712
- Yeah, you were.
- I was late, yeah.

00:02:22.712 --> 00:02:24.930
- Yes. You were.

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:26.373
- Walking stereotype.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:31.083
But I come in there late.

00:02:31.083 --> 00:02:33.090
In a circle area, they're already talking.

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:34.380
And it's a fire and it's snow.

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:36.060
And I'm not, I'm a bay area guy

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:38.400
and I'm like excited
to see snow, you know.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.147
And I sit down in this circle

00:02:42.147 --> 00:02:44.320
and I feel, you know, deeply inadequate

00:02:45.276 --> 00:02:48.120
and I missed everybody
introducing themselves.

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:49.650
So now it's my turn to introduce myself.

00:02:49.650 --> 00:02:51.690
So I fumbled through that,

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:53.340
and then I hear that voice again,

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:56.340
and I see the person
that it's coming out of.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:58.353
And that person was Michelle Satter.

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:01.140
And as soon as she started talking,

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:04.443
you could see like the whole
room's anxiety shift away.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:07.710
And when that Lab started up,

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:08.670
I met all the staff

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:10.860
and everybody who was
working under her there,

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:13.410
they all seemed to kind of
embody that same vision,

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:15.993
that same spirit, was a comforting spirit.

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:19.020
And then I got to meet my Lab mates

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:21.780
and they showed us their movies.

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:23.040
And as soon as those movies rolled,

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:25.090
I felt deeply inadequate again, you know.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:29.160
I was in there with a
guy named David Lowery,

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:30.930
a woman named Mario Heller,

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:32.970
and I was in there with the great,

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:34.350
Chloe Zhao,

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:35.790
and they played her movie.

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:37.560
And I was like, y'all
probably should switch jobs.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:40.440
You know what I mean?

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:43.020
But again, we were mentored there

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:45.300
by incredible screenwriters
and filmmakers.

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:46.560
And we were mentored by each other.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:48.600
We got share our scripts with everybody.

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:49.980
And I started to get the feeling

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:52.950
that maybe that group of
filmmakers might, you know,

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:55.850
might have a serious impact
on the world in this industry.

00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:01.050
Shortly as a Screenwriter's
Lab was winding down

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:02.940
and we were getting ready
to go into the festival.

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:04.110
I had a meeting with Michelle,

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:06.420
where it was just me and her one-on-one.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:08.640
I got to hear that voice
up close and personal,

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:10.110
you know, and it was amazing.

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:11.790
She asked me what I needed for my film.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:13.890
I shared with her that
I was deeply terrified.

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:15.390
I thought I was about to be going.

00:04:15.390 --> 00:04:17.910
And she said, "Well
look, you need a lawyer,

00:04:17.910 --> 00:04:20.610
you need an agent, you need Craig.

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:22.800
And I can help you with
all of those things."

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:23.910
And it wasn't just words.

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:27.000
Within days, I was having
a meeting with my agent.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:28.653
Who my agent now? Craig Castell.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:33.630
I met my lawyer now who's done
all of my deals since then.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:35.057
And she set us up with Craig

00:04:35.057 --> 00:04:36.570
and we were ready to get going.

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:39.600
She was just like a complete
bridge for us who were,

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:41.370
you know, young artists
coming outta school

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:42.570
and coming outta, you know,

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:43.770
never having done this before.

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:45.150
Bridge that's into believing

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:47.280
that we could do
something in this industry

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:48.723
and maintain our voice.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:51.120
Fast forward a few months later,

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:52.830
I shot my film

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:56.040
and I was sharing the
film around with people,

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:57.720
and everybody was giving
me feedback saying

00:04:57.720 --> 00:04:58.800
that they liked it.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:01.110
And then I sent the film to Michelle,

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:03.660
and I'll never forget where
I was when I called her

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:05.100
to get feedback.

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:07.710
I was standing on the
corner in East Oakland.

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:09.630
And I was ready for that soothing voice

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:11.380
to give me all kinds of confidence.

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:14.823
And she tore that film apart, man.

00:05:18.027 --> 00:05:21.000
It was the first time I
heard that voice weaponized.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:22.594
You know what I mean?

00:05:22.594 --> 00:05:25.350
It was like you gotta fix
this and this isn't working.

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:26.250
And maybe change this,

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:27.750
this scene is all a place, you know,

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:29.580
this movie needs to feel
like this, this, this,

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:31.140
that, this, and this ain't working.

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:31.973
And I'm like, man,

00:05:31.973 --> 00:05:34.170
who is this person on the
other side of the phone?

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:35.813
You know? And I, again,

00:05:35.813 --> 00:05:38.400
inadequacy comes back and
I feel like a failure.

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:39.510
I feel like I let Michelle down.

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:41.767
And then the last thing she says to me is,

00:05:41.767 --> 00:05:43.620
"Hey, make sure you get a
good night's sleep tonight.

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:45.413
You sound like you haven't been sleeping.

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:47.730
I've been there, you know,
a good night's sleep.

00:05:47.730 --> 00:05:50.910
You may be a look at things
differently in the morning."

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:52.320
Took her advice.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:53.430
And I went back to the editing

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:54.360
and saw that every single one

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:56.100
of her notes was the real deal.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:57.930
And I realized this was the first person

00:05:57.930 --> 00:05:59.880
who was watching the film
and giving me feedback

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:02.703
that actually cared about
me and cared about the film.

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:04.973
She was incredible.

00:06:04.973 --> 00:06:09.563
And we went to Sundance Film Premieres.

00:06:09.563 --> 00:06:11.730
And leading up into that,

00:06:11.730 --> 00:06:14.246
she made the most incredible introduction

00:06:14.246 --> 00:06:16.873
that she's ever made for me.

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:25.890
We had been been accepted to the festival.

00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:27.937
And she gives me a call and she says,

00:06:27.937 --> 00:06:30.960
"Hey, do you guys have anybody
running your social media?"

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:32.760
And I didn't even have a MySpace.

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:33.593
I didn't have a social media.

00:06:33.593 --> 00:06:37.830
I was very deficient in this category.

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:38.663
And she says, "You know,

00:06:38.663 --> 00:06:40.650
you're gonna need one for your film."

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:42.510
She says, "I'd like you to meet somebody

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:44.130
who I think would be great for you."

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:45.530
Who's our son? Michael Latt.

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:49.380
And Michael was a ex-football
player in high school.

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:50.340
He was a Star Quarterback.

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:52.410
And she knew I played football coming up,

00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:54.090
who was just coming up outta Chapman

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:57.540
and was very proficient
with Social Media Marketing.

00:06:57.540 --> 00:06:59.160
She thought he'd be perfect for us,

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:00.510
and I wanted to meet anybody

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:02.010
who's remotely related to Michelle.

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:03.360
You know what I'm saying? So I was like,

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:04.950
I'll let her sit down with him.

00:07:04.950 --> 00:07:06.660
And Michael just blew us away.

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:09.120
He built the most incredible
social media campaign

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:10.680
for our film before it was even acquired.

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:12.030
Once we was acquired,

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:14.280
the distribution company kept him on,

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:16.050
made him professional at it.

00:07:16.050 --> 00:07:19.050
And he and I got to collaborate
outside of the film ministry

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:20.880
to start "Black of Human Rights"

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:23.310
and do all types of
social justice advocacy

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:26.553
and find an intersection
between the arts and activism.

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:34.320
Michael's work changed the world.

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:36.057
To know him.

00:07:36.057 --> 00:07:38.807
(crowd applause)

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:47.010
To know him,

00:07:47.010 --> 00:07:48.183
was to know Michelle,

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:50.310
was to know his father, David,

00:07:50.310 --> 00:07:51.960
was to know his brother, Frankly.

00:07:54.870 --> 00:07:56.520
And since his life was cut short,

00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:01.743
I've realized to know them,

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:04.623
is to know Michael.

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:10.143
Michelle, you changed our lives.

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:12.030
But you, yeah,

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:15.183
I do believe Michael was your
greatest gift to the world.

00:08:20.143 --> 00:08:21.750
And every time I talk to you,

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:23.350
every time I'm in your presence,

00:08:24.750 --> 00:08:26.650
it feels like he's still here with us.

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:32.400
- Thank you, Ryan.

00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:36.603
Michelle, you're a mother to me.

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:39.543
And you're a mother to Ryan,

00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:45.090
and you're a mother to so many
people sitting in this room.

00:08:45.090 --> 00:08:48.630
You're a mother to all this artist

00:08:48.630 --> 00:08:52.110
that you have nurtured and supported.

00:08:52.110 --> 00:08:54.003
We're so grateful to you.

00:08:55.650 --> 00:08:57.423
We wouldn't be here without you.

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:00.873
We know you're hurting.

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:03.603
We're so sorry.

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:08.110
And we know that there's
nothing we can say

00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:11.223
that can take away the pain.

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:17.720
But we want to say

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:20.883
and we hope we can say this to you,

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:25.623
we are all your children.

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:30.063
We are all your children.

00:09:31.050 --> 00:09:32.609
We love you.

00:09:32.609 --> 00:09:35.359
(crowd applause)

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:47.670
We love you so, so much.

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:50.040
And we will stand by you.

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:50.913
Always.

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:53.370
Always.

00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:55.993
(crowd applause)

00:09:55.993 --> 00:09:58.576
(bright music)

